sm, shaping the genre's future direction."}], "places": [{"name": "St. Petersburg, Russia", "role": "Setting"}, {"name": "Moscow, Russia", "role": "Cultural Hub"}, {"name": "Paris, France", "role": "Literary Influence"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: The Overcoat", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "the-overcoat"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q402673", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Overcoat"], "tags": ["literary work", "Novels & Prose Fiction", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "The Overcoat contributed to the historical record of the Modern period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The Overcoat significantly influenced Russian literature and the development of realism, impacting authors and cultural discourse on identity and class. Its themes resonate in modern discussions about alienation and bureaucracy, reflecting ongoing societal issues.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-02T05:55:56Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28568530456", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "1842 story by nikolai gogol Created by Nikolai Gogol. Created c. 1841.", "newValue": "The Overcoat, a short story published in 1842 by Russian author Nikolai Gogol, serves as a foundational work of modern literature, particularly within the realm of Russian realism. Set in St. Petersburg, it encapsulates the struggles of the individual against the bureaucratic machinery of society in the early 19th century. Gogol's poignant exploration of identity, social status, and existential despair resonates deeply in a world undergoing rapid change, making it a crucial text for understanding the cultural shifts of the time.

The narrative follows Akaky Akakievich Bashmachkin, a lowly government clerk whose life revolves around his old overcoat. The story takes a turn when he decides to purchase a new overcoat, an event that transforms his existence, albeit temporarily, and highlights his desperate desire for recognition and dignity. However, the joy of his new coat is short-lived; after it is stolen, Akaky's efforts to reclaim it lead to his emotional and physical disintegration, symbolizing the indifference of society towards the marginalized. Published during a time of social upheaval in Russia, Gogol’s story captures the essence of the human condition amidst the harsh realities of bureaucratic life.

